#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# spherical_bessel_j

#% text
# `spherical_bessel_j(n, z)` gives the spherical Bessel function j_n(z).

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `spherical_bessel_j(0, z)` is `sinc(z)` = sin(z)/z, with the value 1 at 0, so its domain is every number. For an integer order n >= 1 and a symbolic argument the function stays as it is, odd or even with n (j_n(-z) = (-1)^n j_n(z)): its elementary form in sin(z) and cos(z) has no value at 0, where j_n is 0. That form is what `function_expand` gives, and it is the value at a numeric argument. Other numeric orders give sqrt(pi/(2 z)) times the Bessel function of order n + 1/2.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
spherical_bessel_j(0, x)
#% output
#= sinc(x)

#% cell
spherical_bessel_j(1, x)
#% output
#= spherical_bessel_j(1, x)

#% cell
function_expand(spherical_bessel_j(1, x))
#% output
#= sin(x)/x^2 - cos(x)/x

#% cell
series(spherical_bessel_j(1, x), x, 0, 4)
#% output
#= x/3 - x^3/30 + O(x^5)

#% cell
spherical_bessel_j(2, 1.5)
#% output
#= 0.12734928368840825

#% text
# At a high order and a small argument the upward recurrence loses every digit, so the power series is used (the value is about 7e-49):

#% cell
spherical_bessel_j(20, 0.0703)
#% output
#= 6.627918330870939e-49

